*Sample input for TSFUTL
*Each line to be echoed as a comment or heading line
*must start with an asterisk in column 1.
;   Important:  This utility no longer processes old style 
;   sequential unformatted DTSF's used in version 10.17 of FEQ
;   and earlier.  The UPDATE command, given below, will convert
;   old style DTSF's to the new style direct unformatted form.
;   Thus you must first convert your project to version 10.20 or 
;   later if you wish to use this utility.  
;
;   TSFUTL is a utility program for manipulating TSF files. 
;   This version only works with diffuse time series files, denoted
;   as DTSF.  Later additions might work with point time series
;   files.  When they are added they will be denoted by PTSF
;
;   The input to TSFUTL is broken into commands.  A command line
;   starts with a command and is followed in most cases by 
;   an argument.  For example, one of the commands is PRINT but
;   we must specify what to print.  In this case the only 
;   option is to print the contents of a DTSF.  Thus the
;   complete command line is: PRINT DTSF.  It can also be
;   in lower case: print dtsf.  It can be mixed case in 
;   anyway you see fit.  However, the word PRINT must come 
;   before DTSF and there must be one or more spaces separating them.
;   The command does not need to start in column 1; it can
;   start anywhere on the line.  The only rule is that all 
;   items on the command line must be on one line. 
;
;   The command line initiates a command block.  A command 
;   block is one or more lines that follow the command line
;   and define values needed by the command.  The command block
;   is ended by an END statement on a line followed by the
;   command name.  In this version the command name is not
;   checked but in later versions it will be checked so 
;   don't play loose with the rules!
;
;   This is an example input and in order for it to work 
;   certain things must be true.  This input assumes that
;   the example time series files are stored in a directory
;   with the name TSF.  This directory is placed in the 
;   root directory and is on the same drive as this file.
;   You must have the DTSF file: TSFLONG.SLT present in
;   the TSF directory.  This DTSF will be the starting 
;   point for what this example input does.  This DTSF file
;   is large and we will be creating more large files.  
;   You will need about 25 megabytes of free space on your
;   disk. 
;
;   A bit of review of the history of DTSF's is in order to 
;   understand some of what TSFUTL is doing.  Originally all
;   DTSF's used eight bytes to store runoff values.  However,
;   it soon became evident that was more precision than
;   was needed and a second format for the DTSF's was created.
;   It only requires four bytes for each runoff value.  The
;   difference in results obtained from the two formats
;   is negligible but the size reduction is substantial.
;   Because the differences in results are small TSFUTL will 
;   automatically create the newer format DTSF as a result of 
;   any operation.  That is, the old format is supported in a 
;   read mode only. The initial DTSF used here is in the older 
;   format and will be used to create various other DTSF's to 
;   illustrate the commands in TSFUTL. 
;
;   Another change in the format of the DTSF took place in 
;   version 10.20.  This was a change from using the sequential 
;   unformatted style to the direct unformatted style.  To use 
;   a version of 10.20 or later, the DTSF must be updated.  The
;   version of tsfutl used must be compiled with the same compiler
;   as was used to compile the version of the software that 
;   created the DTSF.  This will often be HSPF and you must 
;   know what compiler was used.  If you cannot determine the 
;   compiler, then try both executables of TSFUTL.  If you get 
;   a strange error or even a system message, then you have picked
;   the wrong exectuable!  This of course assumes you know what
;   you are doing with the input.  The following examples should 
;   work because all of them have been tested one or more times. 
;
;
;   The commands  supported are as follows:
;
;    Command Name  Command Argument   Function
;    ------------  ---------------- ------------------------------------
;      GET          DATES           Scans a DTSF and reports the starting
;                                   and ending date/time strings for each
;                                   event in the DTSF. It also prints 
;                                   a multiplying factor that is used
;                                   to create a character dump of a DTSF
;                                   to move it between computers or 
;                                   compilers that cannot share 
;                                   unformatted data. See the DUMP and
;                                   RESTORE commands below.
;      
;     PRINT         DTSF            Prints part or all of a DTSF in s 
;                                   human-readable form.
;
;     CONVERT       DTSF            Converts an old format DTSF to a 
;                                   new format DTSF. 
;
;     DELETE        EVENTS          Reads events from one DTSF, called the
;                                   old DTSF, and transfers them to a
;                                   new DTSF deleting any events selected
;                                   by the user. 
;
;     MERGE         DTSF            Uses a primary DTSF and one or more
;                                   secondary DTSF's and merges them
;                                   to create a new DTSF.
;
;     DUMP          DTSF            Converts the entire contents of a
;                                   DTSF to a character file, called
;                                   an ASCII file.  The multiplying
;                                   factor from GET DATES is used
;                                   to convert the runoff values to 
;                                   integers between 0 and 999999.
;
;     RESTORE       DTSF            Restores the entire contents of a
;                                   DTSF from an ASCII file created by
;                                   DUMP.
;
;     UPDATE        DTSF            Reads an old style sequential unformatted
;                                   DTSF and creates a new style direct unformatted
;                                   DTSF.
;
;     FINISH                        Defines the end of the input for
;                                   TSFUTL.  
;                              
;
;  The first example is of GET DATES.  Its command block must give
;  the name of the DTSF file to process.  All specification of
;  values must not cross a line boundary.  Names of files cannot
;  have blanks.  The value specifications all have one or more
;  words to the left of an equal sign.  Only one of those
;  words is used by TSFUTL.  If that word does not appear TSFUTL
;  cannot understand what you are talking about!  Other words can 
;  appear to the left of the equal sign to further define what
;  the value is.  These can be selected by the user.  The key word
;  MUST appear and must be separated from any other words by one
;  or more spaces.  The key words will be in all upper case
;  in these examples in order to identify them.  
;
;  The file name can be a fully qualified name including a drive letter
;  just as MS-DOS allows.  The name can be up to 64 characters in length.  
;
;  To run TSFUTL, invoke it like FEQ, that is, with the name of the 
;  executable file followed by two arguments.  The first argument
;  is the name of the file that defines the user input for TSFUTL.
;  That is this file--the one your are reading right now.  I have
;  called this file: INPUT.  Thus to run this example insert a line
;  following  the line with END GET on it that contains the command:
;  FINISH.  That will limit the example to the GET DATES 
;  example.    Then exit this file and get back to the command
;  prompt for the operating system.  Then type
;
;    TSFUTL  INPUT  OUTPUT
;
;  followed by pressing the ENTER key.  If all goes well the 
;  program will indicate what it is working on: GET DATES and
;  when it finishes will report: Normal stop.
;

GET DATES
    FILE name =  TSFLONG.SLT
END GET

;  Now remember to remove the FINISH and move it below the following
;  command example.  You should also move the GET DATES block
;  below the FINISH to prevent it being processed again.  There
;  is no harm other than the computation time taken. From the series 
;  of dates for the various events I have selected an interval to
;  illustrate printing of the DTSF contents. 

PRINT DTSF
   DTSF File name   = tsflong.slt
   START time =1972/ 8/22:  0.000000  'This is the first event in 1972.
   STOP time=  1973/ 1/ 7: 24.
END PRINT


;  The runoff is printed in units of 0.001 inch per hour.  Integers are
;  used to save space in the output.  Each event is headed by its
;  starting and ending time.  The print window, that is the time
;  interval between the START and STOP time values should extend
;  a bit beyond the end of the final event to avoid double printing
;  of the summary statistics.  These statistics are given for each
;  series in each event.  They are the maximum intensity, the 
;  minimum intensity, and the average intensity all in the same
;  units as the runoff values are printed. 
; 
;  The line width for the printed output will exceed 80 or even 132
;  columns.  To see the extra columns you will need an editor that
;  can access more than 80 columns and you may have to shift your
;  display window.  Ideally you should be seeking an operating system
;  and an editor that is not limited to an 80 column window in 
;  any case.  MS Windows NT leaves those restrictions behind.  I
;  do not know if Windows 95 does the same since I have no
;  experience with that OS.  However, with Windows NT and with
;  the Rimstar editor and a 17-inch diagonal screen I can display
;  about 178 columns and still maintain legibility. 
;
;
;  Move the FINISH below the following example of converting
;  to the newer format for a DTSF.  In this case we give
;  two values: the file name for the old format DTSF and
;  the file name for the new format DTSF.

 CONVERT DTSF
    OLD file name= tsfLONG.slt
    NEW file name =TSFSHORT.SLT
END CONVERT

;  The new DTSF file has a stated size of 2,976,275 bytes.  The
;  old format file has a stated size of 7,018,677 bytes.  There
;  is a significant reduction in space required for a DTSF
;  with 25 distinct runoff time series in it!

