;  Now we can combine the two DTSF's created above
;  by using the MERGE command.  The DTSF's being merged
;  should have no overlap in their non-dummy events.
;  That is the time span of any event, excluding the
;  dummy event again, should not overlap any other event.

MERGE DTSF
   PRIMARY file name= tsf1972
   NEW tsf file name= merged.1
   SECONDARY file name= TSF1987
END MERGE

;  Now we try the merge in the other order.
MERGE DTSF
   PRIMARY file name= tsf1987
   NEW tsf file name= merged.2
   SECONDARY file name= TSF1972
END MERGE


;  The order of the secondary files becomes important
;  when more than one is given.  The files cannot backup
;  in time.  That is the sequence of 
;  secondary files should be in order of the sequence
;  of the events they contain, excluding the dummy event
;  in each file.  The file with the latest events 
;  should be last in the list and the file with the
;  earliest events should be first in the list and
;  be the primary file.
;
;
;  There are times when a DTSF must be transferred to
;  another computer that uses a different CPU chip
;  and therefore UNFORMATTED data must be converted.
;  Sometimes utility programs are available to do this
;  but a DTSF is not pure numeric.  Some of the data
;  is character, some is in a floating point form, and
;  some is in integer form.  Each of these must be treated
;  differently in making a direct conversion.  TSFUTL
;  avoids this problem by making an indirect conversion.
;  The same problem may arise when two different compilers
;  are used on the same computer-each compiler designer
;  chooses the detail of keeping track of the internal
;  form of UNFORMATTED data.  Please see the UPDATE 
;  command below if only converting from the old style 
;  sequential unformatted to the new style direct unformatted
;  form of a DTSF.
;
;  Thus assume we want to convert from  A (computer
;  or compiler or both) to  B.  We do the following:
;
;  Step 1. On A run TSFUTL and DUMP a character 
;          form of the DTSF.  Run GET DATES to define a 
;          multiplying factor to use.  Do not take the 
;          multiplying factor as printed at the end of the 
;          GET DATES output because roundoff could cause the 
;          maximum intensity in the DTSF to be falsified.
;          In the above example, GET DATES reports a 
;          factor of 1.07005E10.  Use 1.07E10 instead
;          to make sure that the maximum intensity will
;          not round to a number greater than 999999 when
;          it is multiplied by this factor. 
;       
;  Step 2. Move the ASCII file created by the DUMP
;          command to B.  Also move the source
;          of TSFUTL to B. 
;  Step 3. Compile TSFUTL on B.  This may require some
;          changes to the code.  TSFUTL uses some
;          Fortran 90 extensions that Lahey provided
;          to FORTRAN 77.  The major extension that
;          may not be supported on B is allocatable
;          arrays.  If a Fortran 90 compiler is
;          available on B then there should be little
;          problem. 
;  Step 4. Convert the ASCII file to a DTSF by using
;          the RESTORE command in TSFUTL on B.
;
;  We cannot got through all the steps here but we will
;  simulate the steps by creating an ASCII file from
;  a DTSF and also restore it to a DTSF. 

DUMP DTSF
   DTSF file name= TSFSHORT.SLT
   ASCII file name= dtsfdump
   conversion FACTOR = 1.07E10
END DUMP

;  We then restore the ASCII file to a DTSF with
;  a new name. 

RESTORE DTSF
    ASCII file name = DTSFDUMP
    DTSF file name = testdtsf
END RESTORE


;Example for UPDATE DTSF
UPDATE DTSF
    OLD file name= t3g102505.tsf
    NEW file name= t3g102505.dtsf
END UPDATE


FINISH
